  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: An upper level low digging south from\nthe Gulf of Alaska will approach the Pacific NW coast this \nafternoon, which will bring increasing clouds to much of northern\nNevada along with a slight chance of light rain showers to far \nnorthern portions of Humboldt County. Otherwise dry across the CWA\ntoday and tonight. The approaching system will also produce \nbreezy southwesterly winds across northern and central NV today. \nStrongest winds will be across Humboldt County and northern \nportions of Elko County where gusts of 30 to 40 mph are expected. \nBlowing dust is expected to reduce visibilities at times today \nacross much of Humboldt County, Western Elko County, along with \nportions of Northern Lander and Eureka Counties. Reductions in \nvisibility to 3 miles or less are possible in these areas, \nincluding portions of Interstate-80 at Winnemucca by late this \nmorning, which may expand east toward Carlin and Elko by late this\nafternoon and early evening. Temperatures continue to trend \ncooler today with highs generally in the 70s. Low temperatures \ntonight will drop into the low to mid 40s for most locations, \nthough some of the higher elevation valleys and areas bordering \nmountains such as Ruby Valley east of the Ruby Mountains may see \nlows dip into the low to mid 30s. The upper level low off the Pac\nNW coast today will continue it's SE trajectory tonight into \nThursday. Light rain showers across northern portion of Humboldt \nCounty Thursday will expand slightly south and east Thursday \nnight, with light rain shower activity reaching most of Humboldt \nCounty and far NW portions of Elko County. Winds Thursday \nafternoon will be breezy once again, with slightly higher winds \nexpected across central NV. Winds across east-central NV approach \nlow end advisory levels Friday afternoon, with sustained SW winds \nof 15 to 25 mph and gust to 45 mph in valley locations across \nWhite Pine County, with higher terrain likely reaching advisory \nlevel gusts. Dry across the CWA on Saturday. Moist and unstable \nair is looking more and more favorable for the southeastern third \nof the CWA Sunday afternoon, with showers potentially expanding \nnorth and west Sunday evening and night. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Forecast confidence is high\nregarding a cooling trend today and tonight. Forecast confidence \nis slowly increasing regarding periods of rain late this weekend \ninto early next week. No deviations from the NBM model output.",
